{
  "dom_id": "step-q127295",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,2)$ where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\nT(\\pi )=i,\\,N(\\pi )=-k.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,2)$ is $p=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =i\\times (-k) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi )=j=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(1)+z(0) & =0(0)+1(1)+2(0) \\\\ 0+y+0 & =0+1+0 \\\\ y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
